{"id": "743941004360417341", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:15:57.195000+00:00", "content": "i see", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743941472453263479", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:17:48.797000+00:00", "content": "Also thanks for mentioning `-O4,p`, I’ve just been using `-O4`. Now everything works without the `#pragma`! 🥳", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743941915421966446", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:19:34.409000+00:00", "content": "With `-O4` I had an interesting function that wanted to use `r28-31`, so it would save them with `stmw`, but with the pragma it wouldn’t save them at all 🤔", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743942460824092703", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "shibboleet", "author_id": "519168521918349312", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:21:44.443000+00:00", "content": "i had -Os lol", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743942495876153367", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "shibboleet", "author_id": "519168521918349312", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:21:52.800000+00:00", "content": "I'm guessing SMG is -O4,s", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743943142834962472", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:24:27.047000+00:00", "content": "so -O4,p matches everything?", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743943153459134524", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:24:29.580000+00:00", "content": "<@!170141360375070720>", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743943451166375937", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:25:40.559000+00:00", "content": "Didn’t check everything again yet but at least no more `stmw`s", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743946127753084990", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T21:36:18.707000+00:00", "content": "Yup, everything else I had with -O4 and use_lmw_stmw off still matches with -O4,p", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743967562542481459", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:01:29.159000+00:00", "content": "```cpp\n    unsigned long lang = this->getFlag(0x80000);\n    if (lang == 0) {\n        lang = OSGetLanguage();\n        if ((lang & 0xFF) >= 5) {\n            lang = 0;\n        }\n        this->setFlag(0xA0001, lang & 0xFF);\n    } else {\n        this->setFlag(0xA0001, lang - 1);\n    }```I love how they clamped the OS language to a byte twice, yet the language flag on the memory card was just used as is", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743968526871560301", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:05:19.073000+00:00", "content": "might be compiler generated cast yeah", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743972583883669547", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:21:26.340000+00:00", "content": "Interesting", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743972862259494953", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:22:32.710000+00:00", "content": "`lang` couldn’t be a char tho, I made a memcard exploit that relies on it being 32 bits", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743973437541974028", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:24:49.868000+00:00", "content": "its not, the compiler will plop it into a 32-bit register, but its 8 bit", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743973515270684743", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:25:08.400000+00:00", "content": "ye I tried something like that but couldn’t get it to match that way", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743974081057128508", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:27:23.294000+00:00", "content": "When putting an 8-bit value into a 32-bit register, the compiler would always clamp the value to a single byte tho", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743974178675490916", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:27:46.568000+00:00", "content": "but yeah I see what you mean about two variables sharing a register", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743974528736034886", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:29:10.029000+00:00", "content": "^", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743974553687949403", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:29:15.978000+00:00", "content": "so the compiler's going to respect the EABI first", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743974571580981308", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Revo", "author_id": "198270238691688448", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:29:20.244000+00:00", "content": "before that", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
{"id": "743975063719510048", "channel_id": "736052549001674884", "author": "Noki Doki", "author_id": "170141360375070720", "timestamp": "2020-08-14T23:31:17.579000+00:00", "content": "But in this particular case, the language flag on the memcard is 4 bytes and all 4 get used in game, only the OS language is 1 byte", "reply_to": null, "attachments": [], "reactions": []}
